#!/usr/bin/env bash
# scripts/campfire-docker-files — write the Docker archive's Dockerfile and README.
#
# Called by scripts/build-campfire-archive with the staged Docker context:
#   campfire-docker-files DIR ROUNDHOUSE_SHA CAMPFIRE_SHA SPINEL_VERSION
#
# DIR already holds pack/ (the `spin pack` output: generated C, runtime
# source, native package source, Makefile) and app/ (what the binary reads
# at run time: static assets, public/, db/, config/). This writes the two
# files a downloader reads first, and the image's entry point (boot).
#
# WHY clang. The build is one 12 MB translation unit and nothing else of
# consequence, so it is pure single-core compiler speed: on the same
# trixie image gcc 14 takes 73 s and clang 19 takes 33 s (2026-09-10,
# Apple silicon, arm64 native). The compiler never reaches the runtime
# image, so its apt weight costs the download nothing.
#
# The source archive's README is written by scripts/campfire-archive-files;
# a prerequisite that changes there may need to change here too.

set -euo pipefail

DIR="$1"; ROUNDHOUSE_SHA="$2"; CAMPFIRE_SHA="$3"; SPINEL_VERSION="$4"

# This recipe needs only Docker's bundled frontend. A syntax directive
# would fetch another image from Docker Hub before even reading the build.
cat > "$DIR/Dockerfile" <<'DOCKERFILE'
# ONCE Campfire, transpiled by Roundhouse and compiled by Spinel.
#
# Two stages. The first compiles pack/ — the generated C plus the Spinel
# runtime as source, with a Makefile that needs a C compiler and make and
# nothing else. The second carries only the binary and what it reads at
# run time. clang, not gcc: the build is one 12 MB translation unit, and
# clang compiles it in under half the time.

FROM debian:trixie-slim AS build
RUN apt-get update -qq && \
    apt-get install --no-install-recommends -y clang make libsqlite3-dev libjemalloc-dev libvips-dev && \
    rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*
COPY pack /src
RUN make -C /src -j"$(nproc)" CC=clang

FROM debian:trixie-slim
# ca-certificates: the CA store the binary's OpenSSL verifies against —
# link unfurls, webhooks and Web Push all leave over https, and
# debian:trixie-slim has no store at all.
RUN apt-get update -qq && \
    apt-get install --no-install-recommends -y ca-certificates libsqlite3-0 libjemalloc2 libvips42 ffmpeg && \
    rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*
# The server runs as an unprivileged user, as campfire's own image does
# (uid 1000, named for that image's `rails`).
RUN groupadd --system --gid 1000 campfire && \
    useradd campfire --uid 1000 --gid 1000 --no-create-home --shell /usr/sbin/nologin
WORKDIR /app
COPY app/ ./
COPY --from=build /src/campfire ./campfire
# The SQLite database lives here; mount a volume to keep it across runs.
RUN mkdir -p storage && chown 1000:1000 storage
VOLUME /app/storage
# boot starts as root only to hand /app/storage to uid 1000 — a volume
# created by an earlier image holds root-owned files — then the server
# runs as that user. Started with `--user`, it skips straight to the
# server.
# The archive already carries boot's executable mode; COPY preserves it.
COPY boot ./boot
ENV PORT=3000
EXPOSE 3000
CMD ["./boot"]
DOCKERFILE

cat > "$DIR/boot" <<'BOOT'
#!/bin/sh
# The image's entry point: see the Dockerfile's note above COPY boot.
set -e
if [ "$(id -u)" = 0 ]; then
  chown -R 1000:1000 /app/storage
  exec setpriv --reuid=1000 --regid=1000 --init-groups ./campfire "$@"
fi
exec ./campfire "$@"
BOOT
chmod 755 "$DIR/boot"

cat > "$DIR/README.md" <<README
# ONCE Campfire → spinel, for Docker

[ONCE Campfire](https://github.com/basecamp/once-campfire) — Basecamp's
MIT-licensed chat app — transpiled from its Rails source by
[Roundhouse](https://rubys.github.io/roundhouse/), compiled ahead-of-time
to C by the [Spinel](https://github.com/matz/spinel) Ruby VM, and packaged
so that building it needs Docker and nothing else.

What is in the box: \`pack/\` is the generated C and the Spinel runtime as
source with a Makefile (\`spin pack\` wrote it — a C compiler and \`make\`
build it, no Spinel required); \`app/\` is what the binary reads at run
time — prebuilt static assets, the error pages, the seed; the
\`Dockerfile\` compiles the one and ships the other.

## Provenance

| | |
|---|---|
| roundhouse | \`$ROUNDHOUSE_SHA\` |
| once-campfire | \`$CAMPFIRE_SHA\` |
| spinel | \`$SPINEL_VERSION\` |

Unlike the source archive, this one does not depend on a Spinel checkout:
the runtime it was generated against travels with it.

## Prerequisites

- Docker (or anything that builds a Dockerfile — podman works)

## Build

Compiles the C — one whole-program translation unit, a minute or two on a
fast machine, longer on a slow one — and produces a ~600 MB image:
\`debian:trixie-slim\` plus the CA certificates and the SQLite, jemalloc
and libvips runtime libraries and ffmpeg. libvips resizes the uploads —
attachments, avatars, the account logo — into the variants Rails serves;
ffmpeg makes the video previews. Those two are most of the size: the
binary is about 11 MB.

\`\`\`sh
docker build -t campfire .
\`\`\`

## Run

Serves campfire on http://localhost:3000. The first visit lands on
campfire's own first-run page, which creates the account, the first user,
and the first room. The database is created on demand in \`/app/storage\`;
the named volume keeps it across container restarts.

\`\`\`sh
docker run --rm -p 3000:3000 -v campfire-data:/app/storage campfire
\`\`\`

\`-e PORT=n\` changes the port inside the container; \`-e SPINEL_WORKERS=n\`
caps the OS worker threads (default: one per core).

The server runs as uid 1000, not root. The container starts as root only
to give \`/app/storage\` to that user, so a volume an earlier image
created as root keeps working; \`--user 1000:1000\` skips that step.

The key that signs cookies and links (sessions, sign-in transfer links,
stream names) is generated on first run and kept in \`/app/storage\`
beside the database, so it survives restarts with the volume. To supply
your own instead — several containers sharing one key, or a key kept out
of the data volume — generate it once (\`openssl rand -hex 64\`), keep it,
and pass the same value on every run:

\`\`\`sh
docker run --rm -p 3000:3000 -v campfire-data:/app/storage \\
  -e SECRET_KEY_BASE="\$CAMPFIRE_SECRET" campfire
\`\`\`

An image built from an archive published before 2026-09-28 signed with
an empty key when \`SECRET_KEY_BASE\` was unset: rebuild from a current
archive, or pass the variable.

Push notifications need a VAPID key pair, as Rails' campfire does:
pass \`-e VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=… -e VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=…\` (URL-safe Base64
P-256; \`npx web-push generate-vapid-keys\` makes a pair, as does
campfire's own \`script/admin/generate-secrets\`). Keep the pair stable —
browsers subscribe against the public key. Without it the page carries no
key and browsers never offer to subscribe.

Behind a proxy that terminates TLS (Fly, a load balancer, Kamal's proxy),
the app builds its absolute URLs with the scheme the proxy reports in
\`X-Forwarded-Proto\`, as Rails does, so its links and fetches stay https.

## Without Docker

The Dockerfile is a recipe, not a requirement. On Linux with a C
compiler, \`make\`, \`libsqlite3-dev\`, \`libjemalloc-dev\` and \`libvips-dev\`
(and \`ffmpeg\` on the path for video previews):

\`\`\`sh
make -C pack -j CC=clang
mkdir -p app/storage && cd app && ../pack/campfire
\`\`\`

On macOS with Homebrew's \`sqlite\`, \`jemalloc\` and \`vips\`, the compiler needs
telling where they are: append \`-I\$(brew --prefix)/include
-L\$(brew --prefix)/lib\` to the \`CFLAGS\` line in \`pack/Makefile\`
(the rest of that line is the program's defines — keep them).

## What is not verified yet

Web push delivery is implemented — the service worker and manifest are
served, subscriptions are stored, and each message's delivery is
encrypted and VAPID-signed byte-for-byte as the web-push gem does it —
but a delivery from this binary to a real browser push service (Google,
Mozilla, Apple) has not yet been confirmed. Note that campfire, as
written, deletes a subscription on any OpenSSL error: after a failed
delivery, subscribe again before retrying. Image uploads — attachments,
avatars, the account logo — work, persist under \`/app/storage/files\`,
and are served as the thumbnails Rails would serve (libvips, through the
spinel-ruby-vips package). See the
[project page](https://rubys.github.io/roundhouse/apps/campfire.html).
README
